    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "\"An explosive is a substance that\nhas the capacity for violent decomposition via rapid oxidation, with a\nsudden release of gas and thermal energy.\"",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "D.G. Caldicott & N.A. Edwards: \"The\ntools of the trade: weapons of mass destruction.\"\n\nEmergency Medicine, 14, 3 (September 2002), pp. 240-248.",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Explosions",
      "links": [],
      "level": 1
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Jean Tinguely",
      "links": [],
      "level": 4
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Untitled Project (n.d.)",
      "links": [],
      "level": 6
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "\"Tinguely says he was entranced by the\nidea of throwing a hand-grenade at the Mona Lisa, and he\neven formulated a detailed plan for carrying out his scheme. However,\nthe likelihood of a prison sentence, and the virtually total inactivity\nthis would bring, made him hesitate.\"",
      "links": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "K.G. Pontus Hultén: Jean Tinguely. 'Méta’. London: Thames and Hudson, 1975, p. 35.",
      "links": []
    },
